<Project>

  <!--
    B2. One place for the settings every project shares, so a new project cannot quietly opt out
    of them. A tool that edits system settings should not ship with warnings nobody read.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Four rules are switched off deliberately. Every other analyzer warning is an error, and these
    are listed individually rather than by turning analysis down, so adding a fifth is a visible
    decision rather than a quiet slide.

    CA1859 — "return the concrete type for performance".
      Fights the design on purpose. Returning IReadOnlyDictionary and IReadOnlyList from engine
      APIs is what stops callers mutating collections the engine still owns, and ITweak is
      returned as an interface because that is the entire point of the tweak abstraction. The rule
      targets hot paths; nothing here runs more than a few dozen times per run, so the trade it
      proposes is the wrong way round.

    CA1720 — "identifier contains a type name".
      Hits RegistryValueType.String, RegistryValueType.Binary and PowerPlanEntry.Guid. Those names
      are the JSON schema of the whitelist files and the vocabulary Windows itself uses for
      registry value kinds. Renaming them would either break the shipped whitelists or put the C#
      and the data files out of step, which is worse than the naming smell.

    CA1716 — "member conflicts with a reserved keyword" (Module, in Visual Basic).
      ITweak.Module and IUndoHandler.Module carry the value written to the change log's "module"
      field, which doc 05 section 5.2 fixes. The C# name matching the logged name is worth more
      than VB ergonomics for a Windows-only desktop app.

    CA1822 — "member can be marked static".
      TweakRunner's methods happen not to touch instance state today. It is a service that is
      constructed, injected and will gain dependencies; making it static would bake in the
      opposite and change every call site to get there.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1859;CA1720;CA1716;CA1822</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
